.TH kermit "" "Command"
.SH NAME
kermit \- file transfer utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkermit c\fR|\fBC\fR [\fIbel baud esc line\fR]
.br
\fBkermit s\fR [\fIabdfhilmtx baud line\fR] \fIfile ...\fR
.br
\fBkermit r\fR [\fIbdfhilt baud line\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
kermit -- file transfer utility
Usage : kermit c|C [ bel baud esc line ]
        kermit s [ abdfhilmtx baud line ] file ...
        kermit r [ bdfhilt baud line ]
Options:
	c|C=connect, s=send, r=receive,
 	d=debug, i=image mode, f=no filename conversion, l=tty line,
	b=baud rate, e=escape char, h=host (server) mode, t=tymnet mode,
	m=special mode for sending files to Apple MacIntosh,
 	a=give complete patname for both sending and recieving files,
	x=give complete path name for receiving file only.
.HE
.B kermit
transfers files to and from a remote system using the Kermit protocol
(Columbia University, 1981-1984).
The first command-line argument is a single word whose first letter
selects the mode --
\fBc\fR, \fBC\fR, \fBs\fR, or \fBr\fR
-- and whose remaining letters are single-letter options, run together
with no separating dashes and no spaces; each option that takes a
value (\fBl\fR, \fBe\fR, \fBb\fR) consumes the next command-line word,
in the order its letter appears in the mode word.
For example
.RS
kermit sfb file 9600
.RE
sends
.I file
with filename-case conversion disabled (\fBf\fR), at 9600 baud (\fBb\fR).
.IP \fBc\fR
Connect: turn the local terminal into a terminal emulator attached to
the tty line named by
.B l
(default
.BR /dev/tty38 ,
default speed 2400).
Typing the escape character (default
.BR "^" ,
a literal caret) followed by one more character invokes a local
command:
.B c
or
.B C
hangs up the modem and closes the connection,
.B s
or
.B S
suspends the connection without hanging up, and
.B h
or
.B H
prints a ``still here'' message.
Any other character after the escape character just rings the bell.
.IP \fBC\fR
Same as
.BR c ,
except the next command-line word (after any options and their
values) is sent down the line, followed by a carriage return, before
the terminal emulator starts.
.IP \fBs\fR
Send: transmit each named
.I file
to the remote Kermit.
.IP \fBr\fR
Receive: wait for the remote Kermit to send one or more files and
store them in the current directory.
.IP \fBd\fR
Increase the debugging level by one (repeatable).
.IP \fBi\fR
Image mode: transfer 8-bit data without the usual 7-bit masking and
CR/LF translation.
.IP \fBf\fR
Do not convert filename case (normally lower case is folded to upper
on send and upper to lower on receive).
.IP \fBl\fR
The next word is the tty line to use, instead of the default.
.IP \fBb\fR
The next word is the line speed in baud, instead of the default.
.IP \fBe\fR
The next word's first character becomes the escape character for
.B c\fR/\fBC\fR
mode, instead of the default caret.
.IP \fBh\fR
Host (server) mode: treat standard input and output as the connection
to the remote system (no line is opened) -- used when
.B kermit
is invoked as a login shell or from a getty on a dial-in line.
.IP \fBt\fR
Tymnet mode: pause briefly between incoming data packets.
.IP \fBm\fR
Macintosh mode: send an extra end-of-line character after each packet,
for MacKermit.
.IP \fBx\fR
Leave slashes in file names alone and transmit the directory structure
(normally only the last component of a name is sent).
.IP \fBa\fR
Take file names in pairs: the first name of each pair is used on the
sending side, the second on the receiving side.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/dev/tty38\fR	default communication line
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B login
.SH NOTES
This is the public-domain Columbia University Kermit for UNIX-like
systems, ported to COHERENT by Lauren Weinstein and Charles Fiterman.
.PP
Without
.BR h ,
.B kermit
opens the tty
.I line
itself and assumes it is the local end of the connection; with
.B h
it assumes its standard input and output are already the remote
connection.
